BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8909f52e3b1665c9@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260915T165640Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Supreme Court Upholds Right to Vote by Mail
DESCRIPTION:AUGUSTA\, MAINE\n– Absentee voting by mail will continue ju
 st as it has in prior elections\, announced Secretary of State Shenna Bell
 ows today.  The US Supreme Court rejected the US Postal Service and Trump
  Administration’s quest to change the rules for mailed ballots.  States
  including the State of Maine had filed a lawsuit to challenge the Trump E
 xecutive Order and US Postal Service rule that would have dramatically lim
 ited the ability of clerks to mail ballots to voters.  A lower court bloc
 ked the rule on constitutional grounds\, and the US Supreme Court denied t
 he Trump Administration’s attempt to overturn the lower court.\n“This 
 is good news for voters and for democracy.  Voters can rest assured that 
 they have all of the same options for absentee voting including receiving 
 and returning their ballot by mail that they have had in every other elect
 ion\,” said Secretary of State Shenna Bellows.  “We know that many Ma
 iners – particularly seniors and people with disabilities – appreciate
  the convenience of voting from home\, and we are thrilled that our joint 
 legal efforts with states across the country to protect the right to vote 
 by mail have succeeded.”\nVoters can request absentee ballots utilizing 
 the Secretary of State’s online\nabsentee ballot portal\n\, in writing\,
  or by calling their\nmunicipal clerk\ndirectly. Absentee voting in Maine 
 begins October 5\, and voters should start receiving their ballots in the 
 mail in the first week of October. Voters can track the status of their ab
 sentee ballot via Maine’s\nonline absentee ballot tracking portal\n.\n##
 #\nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos/news/us-supreme-court-upholds-right-vote-mail
URL:https://www.maine.gov/sos/news/us-supreme-court-upholds-right-vote-mail
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f08c6902a4776dc@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260915T165604Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] State of Maine v. James R. Footman Jr.
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
  2026 ME 97 \nDocket: And-25-463 \nArgued: June 2\, 2026 \nDecided: Septem
 ber 15\, 2026 \n \nPanel: STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, LAWRENCE\
 , DOUGLAS\, LIPEZ\, and TAUB\, JJ.  \n \n \nSTATE OF MAINE \n \nv. \n \nJA
 MES R. FOOTMAN JR. \n \n \nTAUB\, J. \n[¶1]  James R. Footman Jr. appeals
  from a judgment of conviction of \nrobbery (Class A)\, 17-A M.R.S § 651 
 (1)(E) (2026)\, and of violation of condition \nof release (Class E)\, 15 
 M.R.S. §  1092(1)(A) (2026)\, entered by the trial court  \n(Androscoggin
  County\, Archer\, J.) after a jury trial.  Footman also appeals from \nhi
 s sentence.   \n[¶2]  The convictions pertain to a robbery on November 11
 \, 2024.  \nFootman contends that the court  clearly erred or abused its d
 iscretion by \nadmitting evidence relating to a similar robbery on Novembe
 r 14 \, for which \nFootman was not on trial.  Because evidence of the Nov
 ember 14 robbery was \nnot probative of the identity of the person who com
 mitted the robbery  on \nNovember 11\, the court erred in admitting it.  B
 ecause we determine that the \n 2 \nerror was not harmless\, we vacate the
  judgment of conviction and do not reach \nthe sentence appeal. \nI.  BACK
 GROUND \nA. Factual Background \n[¶3]  “Viewing the evidence in the lig
 ht most favorable to the State\, the \njury rationally could have found th
 e following facts beyond a reasonable \ndoubt.”  State v. Ellis\, 2025 M
 E 56\, ¶ 3\, 339 A.3d 794. \n[¶4]  On November 11\, 2024\, an individual
  wearing a medical mask and \nblack Nike shoes with white trim walked into
  a Big Apple store on Main Street \nin Lewiston.  The man approached two c
 lerks at the register and demanded that \nthey give him money.  The man  h
 eld a box cutter with a black  and red handle.  \nOne of the clerks initia
 lly gave him twenty dollars.  The man demanded more \nmoney and the clerk 
 handed him another thirty dollars.  The man then left the \nstore.   \n[¶
 5]  On November 16\,  a law enforcement officer interacted with \nFootman 
 and observed that he was\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt
 /2026/26me097.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me097.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
